          <a href="#cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. DIY vs Professional Cost</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          You can seal a cracked asphalt driveway, and in most cases you should — but the sealing is the last step, not the first. Sealer applied over an unfilled crack does not close it, does not stop water, and fails from underneath within a single freeze-thaw season.
        </p>

        <div id="the-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: Fill First, Then Seal
          </h2>

          <p>
            Driveway sealer is a surface coating measured in thousandths of an inch. It is engineered to protect asphalt from ultraviolet light, water intrusion and chemical spills by forming a continuous film across an intact surface. It has no structural filling capacity whatsoever. Poured over a quarter-inch crack, it runs down into the gap, leaves a thin sagging membrane across the opening, and that membrane tears the first time the crack moves.
          </p>

          <p>
            The correct order is repair, cure, then seal. Every crack wider than roughly an eighth of an inch gets a dedicated crack filler. That filler needs its own cure time, typically 24 to 48 hours. Only then does surface sealer go over the top, and at that point it does exactly what it is designed to do: protect the whole surface, repairs included, from the water and UV that would otherwise reopen them.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Why This Order Is Not Negotiable</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Sealing over cracks is painting over rust.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The crack is a path for water to reach the base beneath your asphalt. Water in the base freezes, expands, and lifts the pavement — which widens the crack, which admits more water. A film of sealer stretched over that opening does not interrupt the cycle. It only hides it until the sealer tears, usually by the following spring.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="assess" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Assessing What You Are Dealing With
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not all cracks mean the same thing. Before buying anything, walk the driveway and sort what you see into these categories, because they lead to genuinely different decisions.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What You See</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Means</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What To Do</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hairline surface crazing, under 1/8"</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Normal surface aging</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Seal over it; a filler-and-sealer product handles it</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Single cracks 1/8" to 1/2"</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Routine thermal cracking</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dedicated crack filler, cure, then seal</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cracks wider than 1/2"</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Significant movement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Backer rod plus filler, or cold patch, then seal</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Alligator cracking, interconnected web</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Base failure underneath</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sealing will not fix it; patch or resurface</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Potholes, missing material</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Base failure and water damage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cold patch or permanent patch first</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cracks with weeds growing through</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Open path to the base</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Kill growth, clear the crack fully, then fill</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The One Pattern That Changes Everything</h3>

          <p>
            Alligator cracking — a web of interconnected cracks that resembles reptile skin — is the pattern that should stop you. It is not a surface problem. It means the base underneath the asphalt has failed, usually from water saturation, and the pavement above it is flexing under load without support. Filling and sealing an alligatored area is genuinely wasted effort, because the movement that created the pattern continues underneath whatever you put on top.
          </p>

          <p>
            A small alligatored patch in an otherwise sound driveway can be cut out and patched. If large portions of the driveway show the pattern, you are looking at resurfacing or replacement, and money spent on sealer is money thrown away. Get an honest assessment before committing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="filling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Fill Cracks Properly Before Sealing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Crack filling is unglamorous and it is the part of the job that determines whether the sealing lasts. Rushing it is false economy.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">1</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Clear the crack completely</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Remove all debris, loose asphalt, soil and vegetation. A screwdriver, wire brush or crack cleaning tool works; compressed air or a pressure washer clears the dust. The filler must contact sound asphalt on both walls.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">2</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Kill and remove any growth</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Weeds and grass in a crack have roots below the pavement. Cutting the tops off does nothing. Treat them, wait for die-off, then dig the root mass out of the crack.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">3</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Let it dry completely</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Filler will not bond to a damp crack. After washing, give it a full day of dry weather before filling. Cracks hold moisture longer than the surrounding surface.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">4</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Use backer rod in deep cracks</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">For cracks deeper than about half an inch, press foam backer rod in first. It gives the filler a floor to sit on, prevents you pouring product endlessly into a void, and lets the filler flex properly.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">5</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Fill slightly proud, then level</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Overfill marginally and strike it flush with a putty knife or trowel. Filler shrinks slightly as it cures, and a slightly proud fill settles level rather than dishing below the surface.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">6</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Let it cure fully before sealing</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Typically 24 to 48 hours, longer in cool or humid weather. Check the product label. Sealing over uncured filler traps solvent and produces a soft spot that never hardens properly.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choosing the Right Filler for the Width</h3>

          <p>
            Rubberized crack filler in a pourable bottle or caulk tube handles the routine eighth-to-half-inch range and stays flexible through seasonal movement, which is the property that matters most. For anything wider than half an inch, or for depressions and shallow potholes, a cold patch or permanent patch material compacted into the void is the right product — pourable filler in a large void simply sinks and cracks.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a network of hairline crazing rather than discrete cracks, a combined filler-and-sealer product can genuinely handle the job in one step, filling micro-cracks up to about an eighth of an inch while sealing the surface. That is the one legitimate case where filling and sealing happen together.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="then-seal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sealing Over Repaired Cracks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once the fillers have cured, the driveway is ready to seal like any other. There are two details specific to a repaired surface worth knowing.
          </p>

          <p>
            First, repairs will remain visible. Crack filler cures to a slightly different texture and sheen than the surrounding asphalt, and a coat of sealer over the top reduces that contrast without eliminating it. On close inspection you will always be able to see where the repairs are. This is normal and not a sign of a bad job. Anyone promising invisible repairs on an asphalt driveway is overselling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Second, the repaired areas benefit from particular attention on the second coat. Filler surfaces are less porous than aged asphalt, so sealer sits differently on them, and a slightly more careful pass across the repairs on the second coat evens out the appearance.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Timing the Whole Project</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Budget a long weekend, not an afternoon.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Clean and wash on day one. Fill cracks on day two and let them cure. First coat of sealer on day three, second coat once it is dry to the touch, then 24 to 48 hours before driving on it. Compressing this schedule is the most common reason sealing jobs over repaired driveways fail early.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            On application method: a squeegee is generally the better choice on a repaired driveway, because the surface is by definition irregular and the blade pressure works material into the varied texture more reliably than a sprayed film would.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-not-to" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. When Sealing Is Not Worth It
          </h2>

          <p>
            There is a point at which sealing a cracked driveway is genuinely a waste of money, and the honest advice is to recognise it rather than spend a weekend postponing the inevitable.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Widespread alligator cracking across a large share of the surface — the base has failed and no surface treatment addresses that.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Multiple potholes with visible base material or soil underneath, rather than isolated surface damage.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Sections that visibly flex, dip or rebound when a vehicle drives over them, indicating no support beneath.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Asphalt that has worn so thin that aggregate is loose and comes away underfoot across broad areas.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Edges crumbling inward more than a few inches along most of the driveway’s length.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Any one of these in isolation and in a small area can be cut out and patched, after which sealing the whole driveway makes sense. Several of them across a significant portion of the surface means the driveway needs resurfacing — a new layer of asphalt over the existing one — or full replacement, and sealer applied first will simply be removed along with everything else.
          </p>

          <p>
            The economics are worth stating plainly. Sealing a driveway is inexpensive relative to replacing it, which is exactly why sealing a sound driveway is such good value and why sealing a failed one is such poor value. The product only pays for itself when there is a functional pavement underneath worth protecting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Repairing and Sealing Actually Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            The economics are what make this job worth doing yourself, provided the damage is genuinely surface-level. The materials are inexpensive relative to the labour, which is the opposite of most home improvement work.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Item</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Covers</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Rough DIY Cost</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Crack filler, pourable</td>
