            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Against that, professional crack filling and sealcoating on a standard two-car driveway typically runs several times the material cost, because you are paying for labour, equipment and disposal. The professional advantage is speed, proper equipment and an experienced eye for what the cracking actually means — which is worth something if you are uncertain whether you are looking at thermal cracking or base failure.
          </p>

          <p>
            The honest split is this. Discrete cracks in sound asphalt are well within DIY territory and the savings are real. Widespread alligatoring, sunken sections or potholes exposing base material are worth a professional assessment before you spend anything, because the answer may be that the driveway needs resurfacing rather than repair, and no amount of filler and sealer changes that.
          </p>
