        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            7. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Over Open Cracks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer has no filling capacity. Poured over a crack it sags into the gap and leaves a thin membrane that tears with the first thermal movement, usually within one season. Worse, it looks finished on day one, so the failure arrives as a surprise the following spring. Fill everything over an eighth of an inch first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling Cracks That Are Still Damp or Dirty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Crack filler bonds to clean, dry, sound asphalt on both walls of the crack. Debris, dust or moisture in the crack means the filler bonds to that instead, and it lifts out as a rubbery strip within a year. Clear the crack fully, wash it, and give it a dry day before filling.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pouring Filler Into a Bottomless Crack</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A deep crack will swallow product indefinitely, and filler poured into a deep void cures unevenly and sinks. Foam backer rod pressed in first gives the filler a floor, uses far less product, and lets the fill flex correctly with seasonal movement.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Before the Filler Has Cured</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Uncured filler under a coat of sealer cannot release its solvent. The result is a permanently soft strip along every repair that picks up dirt, prints under tyres and never fully hardens. Give filler its full label cure time, and longer in cool or humid conditions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing a Driveway With Base Failure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Alligator cracking, flexing sections and potholes with soil visible underneath are structural problems. Filling and sealing them is money spent on cosmetics while the underlying failure continues. Get the base assessed; patching or resurfacing first is the only route that makes the sealing worthwhile.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal a cracked asphalt driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, provided the cracks are filled with a dedicated crack filler and allowed to cure fully first. Sealer is a thin surface film with no filling capacity — applied directly over an open crack it sags into the gap and tears within a season. Repair first, cure, then seal the whole surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to fill cracks before sealing a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for any crack wider than about an eighth of an inch. Narrower hairline crazing can be handled by the sealer itself, and a combined filler-and-sealer product covers micro-cracks up to roughly an eighth of an inch. Anything wider needs dedicated filler and its own cure time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after filling cracks can you seal a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically 24 to 48 hours, and longer in cool or humid weather. Check the specific product label. Sealing over filler that has not fully cured traps solvent underneath and leaves a soft strip along every repair that never hardens properly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will driveway sealer fill cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Standard driveway sealer is a protective surface film measured in thousandths of an inch and has no structural filling ability. Some products are marketed as filler-and-sealer combinations and can bridge hairline crazing up to about an eighth of an inch, but nothing wider.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth sealing a badly cracked driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on whether the cracking is a surface problem or a base problem. Discrete thermal cracks in otherwise sound asphalt are well worth repairing and sealing. Widespread alligator cracking, flexing sections or potholes exposing soil indicate base failure, and sealing those is money spent on a driveway that needs resurfacing or replacement.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is alligator cracking and can I seal over it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Alligator cracking is a web of interconnected cracks resembling reptile skin, and it indicates that the base beneath the asphalt has failed, usually from water saturation. Sealing over it does not help, because the flexing that created the pattern continues underneath. A small patch can be cut out and repaired; widespread alligatoring means resurfacing.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

