        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            You can seal a cracked asphalt driveway, and doing so is one of the best-value maintenance jobs available to a homeowner — but only in the right order. Fill every crack wider than an eighth of an inch with a dedicated filler, let it cure for its full 24 to 48 hours, and only then apply sealer over the whole surface.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Sealer applied directly over open cracks does not close them and does not stop water reaching the base. It sags into the gap, forms a thin membrane, and tears with the first thermal movement. The job looks finished the day you do it and fails by the following spring, which is what makes this mistake so common.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Before you buy anything, sort what you are looking at. Discrete thermal cracks are routine and repairable. A web of alligator cracking, sections that flex under a car, or potholes exposing soil are signs the base has failed, and no amount of surface sealer addresses that. Spend the money on the base, then seal what is worth protecting.
          </p>
